[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where does the canonical user identifier originate? → Server-issued (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Server-issued it is. Next question — it decides how much the storage decision matters.

  You said the ID should persist and be readable across the app. What persists alongside it is a separate call, and it's
  the one with security consequences:

  - [1mIdentifier only[0m — store just the account ID (and maybe a display name). It's not a secret, so leaking it is a
    privacy issue rather than an account-takeover one. Every request still has to authenticate some other way, which for
    this app means the question comes back the moment [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is real.
  - [1mIdentifier plus session token[0m — store the credential too, so subsequent requests can authenticate. This is what a
    working app needs, and it makes the storage choice a security decision: anything readable from JavaScript
    (localStorage/sessionStorage) is exposed to any XSS on the page, and the usual answer is an httpOnly cookie the
    client can't read — which means the client-side store holds the ID for display and the cookie holds the credential.

  I recommend [1midentifier only[0m in the client store, with the credential handled as an httpOnly cookie owned by the
  backend. It gives you exactly what you asked for — a real user ID, persisted, readable by future forms — without
  putting a stealable credential in a place JavaScript can read. It also keeps this design honest about the fact that
  the backend contract is still assumed.
